<h2>What decides the interim figure</h2>

<p>Because the court cannot hold a mini-trial at the interim stage, the sworn affidavit of assets, income and expenditure filed by each side carries decisive weight. Courts look for the tell-tale signals: bank credits that dwarf declared salary, EMIs on assets a "penniless" respondent somehow services, school fees paid without visible income, and — on the claimant\'s side — qualifications and past employment that bear on earning capacity. The interim order is deliberately rough justice: enough to sustain the claimant and children at a standard bearing some relation to the matrimonial household, without pre-judging the trial.</p>

<h2>What changes at the final stage</h2>

<p>Three things. First, the burden becomes real: assertions in affidavits must survive cross-examination, and documents must be proved. Second, defences that are irrelevant at the interim stage acquire weight — for instance, the statutory disentitlements in Section 144(4) and (5) BNSS (living in adultery, refusal without sufficient reason to live with the husband, or living separately by mutual consent) are decided on evidence, not allegation. Third, quantum is fixed with more precision: proved income is apportioned with regard to the needs of all dependants, and the final order states its effective date and its relationship with amounts paid under other orders — DV Act monetary relief or Section 24 HMA pendente lite — so that overlapping awards adjust rather than stack.</p>

<h2>Running the two stages well</h2>

<div class="check">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>For claimants:</strong> file a complete, honest affidavit early; document household expenses contemporaneously; seek maintenance from the date of application; and press for the sixty-day disposal of the interim application.</li>
    <li><strong>For respondents:</strong> disclose income candidly — concealment discovered later poisons the final hearing; place liabilities and dependants on record with proof; and comply with the interim order while contesting quantum, since default forfeits sympathy and invites coercive process.</li>
    <li><strong>For both:</strong> keep pleadings consistent across the maintenance case, DV case and matrimonial petition; contradictions between forums are the most common self-inflicted wound in Delhi\'s family litigation.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>After the final order, the statute stays flexible: Section 146 BNSS permits alteration on proof of a change in circumstances — a job gained or lost, remarriage, illness — and Section 147 deals with enforcement and cancellation consequences. Maintenance law aims at a moving target, and both orders are instruments, not verdicts on character.</p>
</div>
